# Threat Modeling with STRIDE
@mastery L3

Security starts before you write code. **Threat modeling** is the practice of
systematically asking "how could this be attacked?" so defenses are designed in,
not bolted on. STRIDE is a checklist of six threat categories.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  U[User] -->|request| S[Service]
  S --> DB[(Database)]
  S --> EXT[Third-party API]
  classDef t fill:#3a1d1d,stroke:#c2724a;
  class S,DB,EXT t;
```

::: callout info
STRIDE = Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of
service, Elevation of privilege. Walk each data-flow arrow and ask which apply.
:::

::: quiz mcq L3
Which STRIDE category best describes an attacker forging another user's identity?
- [x] Spoofing
- [ ] Tampering
- [ ] Repudiation
- [ ] Denial of service
explain: Spoofing is impersonating a principal; tampering modifies data in transit or at rest.
:::

::: grounding
includes: How to enumerate threats against a system using the STRIDE framework.
excludes: Formal risk quantification; specific vendor tooling
know: STRIDE enumerates six categories of threats to walk a data-flow diagram against [stride]
know: Threat modeling is most valuable during design, before code is written [stride]
source: stride | Microsoft STRIDE threat model | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/STRIDE_model
misconception: Threat modeling is a one-time audit | It is an iterative practice repeated as the design evolves
:::

# The OWASP Top 10 in Practice
@mastery L3
@prereq threat-modeling-with-stride

The **OWASP Top 10** catalogs the most critical web application risks. Injection
— especially SQL injection — remains a classic: untrusted input is concatenated
into a query and changes its meaning.

```python
# VULNERABLE: string-built SQL lets input change the query
query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + name + "'"

# SAFE: parameterized query — the driver treats input as data, never code
cur.execute("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?", (name,))
```

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  A[Untrusted input] --> B{Parameterized?}
  B -->|No| C[Injection risk]
  B -->|Yes| D[Treated as data]
```

::: quiz mcq L3
What is the most reliable defense against SQL injection?
- [ ] Escaping quotes by hand
- [x] Parameterized queries / prepared statements
- [ ] Blocking the word SELECT
- [ ] Hiding database errors
explain: Parameterization separates code from data so input can never alter query structure.
:::

::: grounding
includes: Recognizing and preventing common OWASP Top 10 web vulnerabilities, focusing on injection.
excludes: Exhaustive coverage of every category; exploit development
know: SQL injection is prevented by parameterized queries that separate code from data [owasp]
know: Broken access control is the most common category in the 2021 OWASP Top 10 [owasp]
source: owasp | OWASP Top Ten | https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/
:::

# Prompt Injection & the Lethal Trifecta
@mastery L4
@prereq threat-modeling-with-stride the-owasp-top-10-in-practice

LLM-powered systems add a new attack surface. **Prompt injection** is when
untrusted content the model reads (a web page, a tool result, a document) carries
instructions the model then follows. The danger compounds into the **lethal
trifecta**.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Untrusted input] --> X((Agent))
  B[Access to private data] --> X
  C[Exfiltration path] --> X
  X --> R[[Data breach]]
```

::: callout warn
The lethal trifecta: untrusted input + access to private data + an external
communication path. Remove any one leg and the breach becomes far harder.
:::

::: quiz open L4
You are building an agent that reads customer emails and can call internal tools.
Describe one concrete way to break the lethal trifecta for this agent.
rubric: A strong answer removes one leg — e.g. sandboxing tool calls, requiring human approval before egress, or stripping/labelling untrusted content so it cannot issue instructions.
:::

::: grounding
includes: Why agentic LLM systems are vulnerable to prompt injection and how the lethal trifecta enables exfiltration.
excludes: Model training/data-poisoning defenses; provider-specific APIs
know: Indirect prompt injection delivers attacker instructions through content the model retrieves or a tool returns [trifecta]
know: The lethal trifecta combines untrusted input, access to private data, and an exfiltration path [trifecta]
source: trifecta | The lethal trifecta for AI agents (Simon Willison) | https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/
misconception: Prompt injection only comes from what the user types | Indirect injection arrives via retrieved pages, documents, and tool output
:::
